📦 4. Lists

Lists store **multiple values in one variable**.

Lists are **ordered and mutable**.

Example

```python
numbers = [10,20,30,40]
```

---

Access List Elements

```python
print(numbers[0])
```

---

Add Element

```python
numbers.append(50)
```

---

Remove Element

```python
numbers.remove(20)
```

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🗂 5. Dictionaries

Dictionaries store data in **key-value pairs**.

Example

```python
student = {
    "name": "Saakshi",
    "age": 22,
    "course": "CSE"
}
```

---

Access Dictionary Value

```python
print(student["name"])
```

---

Add New Value

```python
student["city"] = "Delhi"
```

---

Get All Keys

```python
print(student.keys())
```

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

📌 6. Tuples

Tuples are similar to lists but **immutable**.

This means values **cannot be changed after creation**.

Example

```python
numbers = (10,20,30)
```

---

Access Tuple Element

```python
print(numbers[1])
```

---

Tuple Advantages

* Faster than lists
* Safe from modification
* Useful for fixed data

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🔗 7. Sets

Sets store **unique values only**.

Duplicates are automatically removed.

Example

```python
numbers = {1,2,3,4,4,5}
print(numbers)
```

Output

```
{1,2,3,4,5}
```

---

Add Element

```python
numbers.add(6)
```

---

Remove Element

```python
numbers.remove(2)
```

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

📊 Comparison of Python Data Structures

| Type       | Ordered | Mutable | Allows Duplicates |
| ---------- | ------- | ------- | ----------------- |
| List       | Yes     | Yes     | Yes               |
| Tuple      | Yes     | No      | Yes               |
| Set        | No      | Yes     | No                |
| Dictionary | Yes     | Yes     | Keys unique       |

